              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Patterns**\n\nOkay, I'm noticing a distinct pattern. The provided 5x5 grid seems to be segmented into 2x2 blocks by rows and columns filled with zeros. This grid is effectively partitioned into individual blocks, separated by these zero rows and columns. I think identifying these blocks is going to be important.\n\n\n**Discovering Symmetric Relations**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I can see a distinct symmetrical relationship within the grid blocks! Reflecting them horizontally and vertically explains each quadrant's arrangement. The 5x5 grid demonstrates this clearly. Now, a larger 11x11 grid also appears to follow this partitioning into 3x3 blocks organized in a 3x3 layout, and the symmetry is maintained! I need to determine how these relate to the final outputs.\n\n\n**Confirming Symmetry Patterns**\n\nI've been examining the outputs for an 11x11 grid and the crucial feature seems to be an 8-way and 4-way symmetry! I'm verifying this pattern by carefully checking horizontal, vertical, and even diagonal symmetry. Now, I'm theorizing that the output is simply a union of all possible rotations and reflections of the input. I am checking an example with the train 1 input to confirm.\n\n\n**Verifying Transformations**\n\nI'm validating the reflection approach (horizontal plus vertical, or D2). Analyzing Train 2, I confirmed the pattern. Input top-right becomes output top-left through reflection. Mid-left mirrors to mid-right as well. I'm focusing on why a center block is added when the input is sparse. I've noted that the output includes a center column where the input is all zero!\n\n\n**Verifying Symmetries**\n\nI've just had a major breakthrough and validated the D4 symmetry (dihedral group of the square). I've confirmed that the output is simply the union of all 8 symmetries (reflections along horizontal, vertical, main diagonal, anti-diagonal, and rotations) of the input, with a max or non-zero operation. Train 1 output now appears fully D4 symmetric, which validates my theory. I am now exploring its implications.\n\n\n",
